<DocsLayout title="Adding Your First Repo — Band Docs">
  <h1>Adding Your First Repo</h1>

  <p>
    A repo in Band is a registered git repository. Once you add a repo, Band tracks it
    and lets you create worktrees (isolated git worktrees) from it. You need at least one
    repo before you can create worktrees or dispatch tasks.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>

  <p>
    The directory you register must be a <strong>git repository</strong>. Band uses git
    worktrees under the hood, so it needs a valid <code>.git</code> directory to work with.
    The repository can be any size and can have any branch structure.
  </p>

  <h2>Add a repo via the dashboard</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Open the Band dashboard.</li>
    <li>Click the <strong>"Add Repo"</strong> button in the sidebar.</li>
    <li>
      In the <strong>Register Repo</strong> dialog, type the path to your
      git repository, or click the folder icon to browse for it.
    </li>
    <li>Click <strong>Add Repo</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="my-8 mx-auto max-w-md overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border shadow-lg">
    <img
      src="/screenshot-register-repo.png"
      alt="The Register Repo dialog in the Band dashboard, with a Repository path field and a folder-picker icon."
      width="430"
      height="239"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      class="block h-auto w-full"
    />
    <figcaption class="border-t border-border bg-card/50 px-4 py-2 text-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The Register Repo dialog &mdash; type the repository path or click the folder icon to browse.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The repo appears in the sidebar immediately. Band detects the repo
    name from the repository's directory name, picks up the default branch
    (usually <code>main</code> or <code>master</code>), and displays the number
    of existing worktrees.
  </p>

  <h2>Add a repo via the CLI</h2>

  <p>
    You can also register a repo from your terminal using the Band CLI:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band repos add /path/to/your/repo</code></pre>

  <p>For example, to register a repo located at <code>~/code/my-app</code>:</p>

  <pre><code>band repos add ~/code/my-app</code></pre>

  <p>
    You can optionally assign a label to the repo to help organize your repos:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band repos add ~/code/my-app --label "work"</code></pre>

  <p>
    To verify the repo was added, list all registered repos:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band repos list</code></pre>

  <p>
    This outputs a tab-separated list showing each repo's name, path, and worktree count.
    For structured output, add the <code>--output json</code> flag:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band repos list --output json</code></pre>

  <h2>What happens when you add a repo</h2>

  <p>When you register a repo, Band does the following:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Validates the repository</strong> &mdash; Confirms the path points to a valid
      git repository with a <code>.git</code> directory.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Detects the default branch</strong> &mdash; Identifies the main branch
      (e.g., <code>main</code>, <code>master</code>) so worktrees can be branched from it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Discovers existing worktrees</strong> &mdash; If the repository already has git
      worktrees, Band picks them up and displays them as worktrees.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reads repo configuration</strong> &mdash; If a <code>.band/config.json</code>
      file exists in the repository, Band reads it for setup and teardown scripts that run
      when worktrees are created or removed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Removing a repo</h2>

  <p>
    If you want to unregister a repo, you can do so from the CLI:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band repos remove my-app</code></pre>

  <p>
    This removes the repo from Band's registry but does <strong>not</strong> delete the
    repository or any of its files from disk.
  </p>

  <h2>Next step</h2>

  <p>
    With a repo registered, you are ready to
    <a href="/docs/first-worktree">create your first worktree</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
